ry stuff raise  the children make the food but they also  had to you know teach school and their  their their journals are remarkable just  for the amount of complaint and you know  they had reason to complain they had  very hard lives but so if you want if  you want picnic memoirs I would say the  Hawaiian missionaries are probably not  your go-to source but they are  interesting Folsom California you're on  with author and journalist Sarah Vowell  Mike and I read a night you read called  SF nation vacation and I was really  intrigued with the story you told about  Lewis Powell and something that I didn't  understand is that as I were calling  your book they found his skull in a box  in the National Archives or in some  repository was wondering how did it  happen that his skull ended up in a box  and for 150 years nobody thought you  know that's a little odd what is this  well what happened Lewis Powell was one  of the conspirators in the Lincoln  assassination and what happens is if  you're one of the people who plot to  kill the president and other high  government officials and you're executed  they're a little loosey-goosey with what  happens to your remains  so Lewis Powell who was executed for his  part in that conspiracy but I remember  correctly he's the one who stabbed  Secretary of State Seward Powell was you  know hanged along with some of the other  conspirators and somehow his skull ended  up in the Smithsonian amongst all of the  American Indian remains and when the law  was passed to start repatriating those  American Indian remains back to their  tribes the researcher who is going  through all of these bones in the  Smithsonian had I think worked at Ford's  Theater Museum or one of the other  associate historic sites and recognized  Powell's name and so eventually that  skull made it back to Florida where and  was buried near Powell's mother so  that's just one of those strange strange  things so if you want your remains to be  intact  and you know 